# verdict: satisfiable
# Source: ty mdtest typed_dict.md:4498
#   `TypedDict` / Generic `TypedDict` / Constructor inference from nested values
# ty: the intended type of `Outer(inner=Inner(value=1), marker="x")` is
# Outer[int | str] (TODO). ty currently reveals Outer[Unknown].
#
#   class Inner[T](TypedDict):
#       value: T
#   class Outer[T](TypedDict):
#       inner: Inner[T]
#       marker: T
#   Outer(inner=Inner(value=1), marker="x")
#
# Each constructor call gives its T a fresh metavariable (?I inner, ?O
# outer). Each keyword argument is one assertion against its field type.
# The inner result Inner[?I] must fit the outer field Inner[?O]. T is
# invariant, so ?I must equal ?O. ?I holds int from the inner call, and ?O
# holds str from the outer call, so both must equal int | str. A solver that
# fixes ?I = int at the inner call cannot satisfy the outer call. The
# intended type is an upper bound on the outer result.
#
# Variance: a TypedDict field is mutable, so ty treats T as invariant in
# both classes.
# Approximations: each TypedDict is a generic class, and each constructor
# call is a check of its keyword arguments against the field types. The
# literals 1 and "x" are widened to int and str, as ty does for TypedDict
# constructor inference.
class int;
class str;
class Inner[T];
class Outer[T];
metavar ?I;
metavar ?O;

assert inner_value: int <: ?I;
assert outer_inner: Inner[?I] <: Inner[?O];
assert outer_marker: str <: ?O;
assert outer_result: Outer[?O] <: Outer[int | str];
